{"metadata": {"file_suffix": "pdf", "producer": {"name": "mineru", "version": "4.0.10"}, "document": {"page_count": 2145, "page_count_kind": "physical"}}, "extensions": {"mineru": {"tier": "standard", "parse_mode": "txt"}, "docvortex_layout": {"version": 1, "pages": [{"page_idx": 218, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 219, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 220, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 221, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 222, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 223, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 224, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 225, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 226, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 227, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 228, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 229, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}]}}, "pages": [{"page_idx": 218, "blocks": [{"type": "footer", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.142, 0.85, 0.233, 0.867], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Figure 3.28"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.141, 0.079, 0.842, 0.121], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "1000. However, with each additional database there are diminishing returns and increased memory and computation costs.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.218, 0.187, 0.414, 0.204], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Disjoint pattern databases", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.139, 0.27, 0.857, 0.621], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "One might wonder whether the heuristics obtained from the 1-2-3-4 database and the 5-6-7-8 could be added, since the two subproblems seem not to overlap. Would this still give an admissible heuristic? The answer is no, because the solutions of the 1-2-3-4 subproblem and the 5-6-7-8 subproblem for a given state will almost certainly share some moves—it is unlikely that 1-2-3-4 can be moved into place without touching 5-6-7-8, and vice versa. But what if we don’t count those moves—what if we don’t abstract the other tiles to stars, but rather make them disappear? That is, we record not the total cost of solving the 1-2-3-4 subproblem, but just the number of moves involving 1-2-3-4. Then the sum of the two costs is still a lower bound on the cost of solving the entire problem. This is the idea behind disjoint pattern databases. With such databases, it is possible to solve random 15-puzzles in a few milliseconds—the number of nodes generated is reduced by a factor of 10,000 compared with the use of Manhattan distance. For 24-puzzles, a speedup of roughly a factor of a million can be obtained. Disjoint pattern databases work for sliding-tile puzzles because the problem can be divided up in such a way that each move affects only one subproblem— because only one tile is moved at a time.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.14, 0.647, 0.622, 0.669], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "3.6.4 Generating heuristics with landmarks"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.139, 0.683, 0.849, 0.82], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "There are online services that host maps with tens of millions of vertices and find costoptimal driving directions in milliseconds (Figure 3.28 ). How can they do that, when the", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "best search algorithms we have considered so far are about a million times slower? There are many tricks, but the most important one is precomputation of some optimal path costs. Although the precomputation can be time-consuming, it need only be done once, and then can be amortized over billions of user search requests.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}]}, {"page_idx": 219, "blocks": [{"type": "image", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.289, 0.074, 0.855, 0.306], "content": [{"type": "image_body", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.289, 0.074, 0.855, 0.306], "content": ""}, {"type": "image_caption", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.141, 0.317, 0.663, 0.334], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "A Web service providing driving directions, computed by a search algorithm."}]}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.218, 0.403, 0.339, 0.42], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Precomputation"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.139, 0.485, 0.836, 0.553], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "We could generate a perfect heuristic by precomputing and storing the cost of the optimal path between every pair of vertices. That would take", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "O ( | V | ^ { 2 } )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "space, and", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "O ( | E | ^ { 3 } )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "time— practical for graphs with 10 thousand vertices, but not 10 million.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.139, 0.58, 0.854, 0.743], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "A better approach is to choose a few (perhaps 10 or 20) landmark points from the", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "16 "}, {"type": "text", "content": "vertices. Then for each landmark and for each other vertex in the graph, we compute", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "L v "}, {"type": "text", "content": "and store", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "C ^ { * } ( v , L )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "the exact cost of the optimal path from to (We also need", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " v L. "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "C ^ { * } ( L , v ) ;"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "on an undirected graph this is the same as", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "C ^ { * } ( v , L )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "on a directed graph—e.g., with one-way", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "; "}, {"type": "text", "content": "streets—we need to compute this separately.) Given the stored", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "C ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "tables, we can easily create an efficient (although inadmissible) heuristic: the minimum, over all landmarks, of the cost of getting from the current node to the landmark, and then to the goal:", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "equation", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.332, 0.798, 0.664, 0.824], "content": "\\left| h _ {L} (n) = \\min _ {L \\in \\text {Landmarks}} C ^ {*} (n, L) + C ^ {*} (L, \\text {goal}) \\right|"}, {"type": "page_footnote", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.141, 0.769, 0.484, 0.784], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "16 Landmark points are sometimes called “pivots” or “anchors.”"}]}, {"type": "footer", "index": 7, "bbox": [0.218, 0.887, 0.341, 0.904], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Landmark point"}]}]}, {"page_idx": 220, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.139, 0.079, 0.851, 0.24], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "If the optimal path happens to go through a landmark, this heuristic will be exact; if not it is inadmissible—it overestimates the cost to the goal. In an", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\boldsymbol { A } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "search, if you have exact heuristics, then once you reach a node that is on an optimal path, every node you expand from then on will be on an optimal path. Think of the contour lines as following along this optimal path. The search will trace along the optimal path, on each iteration adding an action with cost to get to a result state whose -value will be less, meaning that the total", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "c h c "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "f   =   g + h"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "score will remain constant at", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "C ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "all along the path.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.138, 0.269, 0.849, 0.382], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Some route-finding algorithms save even more time by adding shortcuts—artificial edges in the graph that define an optimal multi-action path. For example, if there were shortcuts predefined between all the 100 biggest cities in the U.S., and we were trying to navigate from the Berkeley campus in California to NYU in New York, we could take the shortcut between Sacramento and Manhattan and cover 90% of the path in one action.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.218, 0.448, 0.294, 0.464], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Shortcuts"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.14, 0.531, 0.799, 0.572], "content": [{"type": "equation_inline", "content": "h _ { L } ( n )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "is efficient but not admissible. But with a bit more care, we can come up with a heuristic that is both efficient and admissible:", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "equation", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.322, 0.597, 0.675, 0.622], "content": "h _ {D H} (n) = \\max _ {L \\in \\text {Landmarks}} | C ^ {*} (n, L) - C ^ {*} (\\text {goal}, L) |"}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.139, 0.649, 0.855, 0.834], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "This is called a differential heuristic (because of the subtraction). Think of this with a landmark that is somewhere out beyond the goal. If the goal happens to be on the optimal path from to the landmark, then this is saying “consider the entire path from to", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "n n "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "L ,"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "then subtract off the last part of that path, from goal to giving us the exact cost of the path from", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "L, "}, {"type": "text", "content": "to goal.” To the extent that the goal is a bit off of the optimal path to the landmark, the", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "n "}, {"type": "text", "content": "heuristic will be inexact, but still admissible. Landmarks that are not out beyond the goal will not be useful; a landmark that is exactly halfway between and goal will give", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "n "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "h _ { D H } = 0"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "which is not helpful.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "footer", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.218, 0.899, 0.373, 0.917], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Differential heuristic"}]}]}, {"page_idx": 221, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.139, 0.136, 0.852, 0.366], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "There are several ways to pick landmark points. Selecting points at random is fast, but we get better results if we take care to spread the landmarks out so they are not too close to each other. A greedy approach is to pick a first landmark at random, then find the point that is furthest from that, and add it to the set of landmarks, and continue, at each iteration adding the point that maximizes the distance to the nearest landmark. If you have logs of past search requests by your users, then you can pick landmarks that are frequently requested in searches. For the differential heuristic it is good if the landmarks are spread around the perimeter of the graph. Thus, a good technique is to find the centroid of the graph, arrange pie-shaped wedges around the centroid, and in each wedge select the", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "k "}, {"type": "text", "content": "vertex that is farthest from the center.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.14, 0.396, 0.854, 0.485], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Landmarks work especially well in route-finding problems because of the way roads are laid out in the world: a lot of traffic actually wants to travel between landmarks, so civil engineers build the widest and fastest roads along these routes; landmark search makes it easier to recover these routes.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.141, 0.513, 0.484, 0.534], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "3.6.5 Learning to search better"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.218, 0.6, 0.375, 0.616], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Metalevel state space", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.218, 0.696, 0.391, 0.713], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Object-level state space", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.139, 0.779, 0.856, 0.916], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "We have presented several fixed search strategies—breadth-first, A*, and so on—that have been carefully designed and programmed by computer scientists. Could an agent learn how to search better? The answer is yes, and the method rests on an important concept called the metalevel state space. Each state in a metalevel state space captures the internal (computational) state of a program that is searching in an ordinary state space such as the map of Romania. (To keep the two concepts separate, we call the map of Romania an", "styles": ["bold"]}]}]}, {"page_idx": 222, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.139, 0.079, 0.853, 0.216], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "object-level state space.) For example, the internal state of the A* algorithm consists of the current search tree. Each action in the metalevel state space is a computation step that alters the internal state; for example, each computation step in A* expands a leaf node and adds its successors to the tree. Thus,", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000F32C.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000F375", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Figure 3.18", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": ", which shows a sequence of larger and larger", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000F32C.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000F375", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "search trees, can be seen as depicting a path in the metalevel state space where each state on the path is an object-level search tree.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.139, 0.245, 0.845, 0.383], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Now, the path in", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000F32C.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000F375", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Figure 3.18", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "has five steps, including one step, the expansion of Fagaras,", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "that is not especially helpful. For harder problems, there will be many such missteps, and a metalevel learning algorithm can learn from these experiences to avoid exploring unpromising subtrees. The techniques used for this kind of learning are described in", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000013FE4.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000013FE4", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapter 22", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": ". The goal of learning is to minimize the total cost of problem solving, trading", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000F32C.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000F375", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "off computational expense and path cost.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.217, 0.448, 0.359, 0.465], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Metalevel learning", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.141, 0.529, 0.604, 0.551], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "3.6.6 Learning heuristics from experience"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.14, 0.564, 0.85, 0.606], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "We have seen that one way to invent a heuristic is to devise a relaxed problem for which an optimal solution can be found easily. An alternative is to learn from experience.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.139, 0.611, 0.856, 0.796], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "“Experience” here means solving lots of 8-puzzles, for instance. Each optimal solution to an 8-puzzle problem provides an example (goal, path) pair. From these examples, a learning algorithm can be used to construct a function that can (with luck) approximate the true", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "h "}, {"type": "text", "content": "path cost for other states that arise during search. Most of these approaches learn an imperfect approximation to the heuristic function, and thus risk inadmissibility. This leads to an inevitable tradeoff between learning time, search run time, and solution cost. Techniques for machine learning are demonstrated in", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000001316E.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001316E", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapter 19", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": ". The reinforcement learning", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "methods described in", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000013FE4.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000013FE4", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapter 22", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "are also applicable to search.", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000001316E.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001316E", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.139, 0.825, 0.85, 0.915], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Some machine learning techniques work better when supplied with features of a state that are relevant to predicting the state’s heuristic value, rather than with just the raw state description. For example, the feature “number of misplaced tiles” might be helpful in predicting the actual distance of an 8-puzzle state from the goal. Let’s call this feature", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " x1(n)."}]}]}, {"page_idx": 223, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.14, 0.079, 0.855, 0.193], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "We could take 100 randomly generated 8-puzzle configurations and gather statistics on their actual solution costs. We might find that when", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "x _ { 1 } ( n )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "is 5, the average solution cost is around 14, and so on. Of course, we can use multiple features. A second feature", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "x _ { 2 } ( n )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "might be “number of pairs of adjacent tiles that are not adjacent in the goal state.” How should", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "x _ { 1 } ( n )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "and", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "x _ { 2 } ( n )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "be combined to predict", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "h ( n ) ?"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "A common approach is to use a linear combination:", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "equation", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.399, 0.216, 0.598, 0.234], "content": "h (n) = c _ {1} x _ {1} (n) + c _ {2} x _ {2} (n)."}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.218, 0.299, 0.279, 0.313], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Feature"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.139, 0.381, 0.837, 0.493], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The constants", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "c _ { 1 }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "and", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "c _ { 2 }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "are adjusted to give the best fit to the actual data across the randomly generated configurations. One expects both", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "c _ { 1 }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "and", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "c _ { 2 }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "to be positive because misplaced tiles and incorrect adjacent pairs make the problem harder to solve. Notice that this heuristic satisfies the condition", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "h ( n )   =   0"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "for goal states, but it is not necessarily admissible or consistent.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}]}, {"page_idx": 224, "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.141, 0.127, 0.278, 0.152], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Summary"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.139, 0.166, 0.858, 0.328], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "This chapter has introduced search algorithms that an agent can use to select action sequences in a wide variety of environments—as long as they are episodic, single-agent, fully observable, deterministic, static, discrete, and completely known. There are tradeoffs to be made between the amount of time the search takes, the amount of memory available, and the quality of the solution. We can be more efficient if we have domain-dependent knowledge in the form of a heuristic function that estimates how far a given state is from the goal, or if we precompute partial solutions involving patterns or landmarks.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.153, 0.356, 0.797, 0.374], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Before an agent can start searching, a well-defined problem must be formulated.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.154, 0.38, 0.845, 0.421], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "A problem consists of five parts: the initial state, a set of actions, a transition model describing the results of those actions, a set of goal states, and an action cost function.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.154, 0.428, 0.848, 0.469], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The environment of the problem is represented by a state space graph. A path through the state space (a sequence of actions) from the initial state to a goal state is a solution.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.154, 0.475, 0.844, 0.516], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Search algorithms generally treat states and actions as atomic, without any internal structure (although we introduced features of states when it came time to do learning).", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.154, 0.522, 0.805, 0.564], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Search algorithms are judged on the basis of completeness, cost optimality, time complexity, and space complexity.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 7, "bbox": [0.154, 0.57, 0.828, 0.635], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Uninformed search methods have access only to the problem definition. Algorithms build a search tree in an attempt to find a solution. Algorithms differ based on which node they expand first:", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 8, "bbox": [0.169, 0.64, 0.834, 0.707], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "– BEST-FIRST SEARCH selects nodes for expansion using to an evaluation function. – BREADTH-FIRST SEARCH expands the shallowest nodes first; it is complete, optimal for unit action costs, but has exponential space complexity.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 9, "bbox": [0.17, 0.712, 0.811, 0.754], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "– UNIFORM-COST SEARCH expands the node with lowest path cost, and is", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "g(n), "}, {"type": "text", "content": "optimal for general action costs.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 10, "bbox": [0.17, 0.76, 0.825, 0.825], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "– DEPTH-FIRST SEARCH expands the deepest unexpanded node first. It is neither complete nor optimal, but has linear space complexity. Depth-limited search adds a depth bound.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 11, "bbox": [0.169, 0.831, 0.854, 0.92], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "– ITERATIVE DEEPENING SEARCH calls depth-first search with increasing depth limits until a goal is found. It is complete when full cycle checking is done, optimal for unit action costs, has time complexity comparable to breadth-first search, and has linear space complexity.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}]}, {"page_idx": 225, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.169, 0.079, 0.831, 0.12], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "– BIDIRECTIONAL SEARCH expands two frontiers, one around the initial state and one around the goal, stopping when the two frontiers meet.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.154, 0.127, 0.824, 0.191], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Informed search methods have access to a heuristic function", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "h ( n )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "that estimates the cost of a solution from They may have access to additional information such as", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "n. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "pattern databases with solution costs.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.169, 0.198, 0.834, 0.238], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "– GREEDY BEST-FIRST SEARCH expands nodes with minimal", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "h ( n )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "It is not optimal", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": ". "}, {"type": "text", "content": "but is often efficient.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.17, 0.245, 0.852, 0.31], "content": [{"type": "equation_inline", "content": "- \\mathbf { A } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "SEARCH expands nodes with minimal", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "f ( n ) = g ( n ) + h ( n ) . \\mathrm { A } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "is complete and optimal, provided that", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "h ( n )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " is admissible. The space complexity of "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "A ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "is still an issue for many problems.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.171, 0.316, 0.764, 0.333], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "– BIDIRECTIONAL", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathbf { A } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "SEARCH is sometimes more efficient than", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\boldsymbol { \\Lambda } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "itself.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.17, 0.34, 0.823, 0.381], "content": [{"type": "equation_inline", "content": "- \\mathbf { I } \\mathbf { D } \\mathbf { A } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "(iterative deepening", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\boldsymbol { A } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "search) is an iterative deepening version of", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\boldsymbol { \\Lambda } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": ", and thus adresses the space complexity issue.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.17, 0.387, 0.834, 0.453], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "– RBFS (recursive best-first search) and", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathbf { S M A ^ { * } }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "(simplified memory-bounded", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathbf { A } ^ { * } )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "are robust, optimal search algorithms that use limited amounts of memory; given enough time, they can solve problems for which", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "A ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "runs out of memory.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 7, "bbox": [0.17, 0.459, 0.842, 0.523], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "– BEAM SEARCH puts a limit on the size of the frontier; that makes it incomplete and suboptimal, but it often finds reasonably good solutions and runs faster than complete searches.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 8, "bbox": [0.17, 0.53, 0.856, 0.571], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "– WEIGHTED", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathbf { A } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "search focuses the search towards a goal, expanding fewer nodes, but sacrificing optimality.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 9, "bbox": [0.154, 0.577, 0.855, 0.668], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The performance of heuristic search algorithms depends on the quality of the heuristic function. One can sometimes construct good heuristics by relaxing the problem definition, by storing precomputed solution costs for subproblems in a pattern database, by defining landmarks, or by learning from experience with the problem class.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}]}, {"page_idx": 226, "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.141, 0.126, 0.633, 0.151], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Bibliographical and Historical Notes"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.139, 0.166, 0.85, 0.304], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The topic of state-space search originated in the early years of AI. Newell and Simon’s work on the Logic Theorist (1957) and GPS (1961) led to the establishment of search algorithms as the primary tool for 1960s AI researchers and to the establishment of problem solving as the canonical AI task. Work in operations research by", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001566A", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Richard Bellman (1957)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "showed the", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000016277", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "importance of additive path costs in simplifying optimization algorithms. The text by Nils Nilsson (1971) established the area on a solid theoretical footing.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.139, 0.333, 0.849, 0.517], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The 8-puzzle is a smaller cousin of the 15-puzzle, whose history is recounted at length by", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000165A6", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Slocum and Sonneveld (2006)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ". In 1880, the 15-puzzle attracted broad attention from the public and mathematicians (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015E21", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Johnson and Story, 1879", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ";", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000016651", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Tait, 1880", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "). The editors of the American Journal of Mathematics stated, “The ‘15’ puzzle for the last few weeks has been prominently before the American public, and may safely be said to have engaged the attention of nine out of ten persons of both sexes and all ages and conditions of the community,” while the Weekly News-Democrat of Emporia, Kansas wrote on March 12, 1880 that “It has become literally an epidemic all over the country.”", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.139, 0.546, 0.853, 0.66], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The famous American game designer Sam Loyd falsely claimed to have invented the 15 puzzle (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001609D", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Loyd, 1959", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "); actually it was invented by Noyes Chapman, a postmaster in Canastota, New York, in the mid-1870s (although a generic patent covering sliding blocks was granted to Ernest Kinsey in 1878).", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000163DD", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Ratner and Warmuth (1986)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "showed that the general version of the 15-puzzle belongs to the class of NP-complete problems.", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "n × n"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.139, 0.689, 0.854, 0.92], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Rubik’s Cube was of course invented in 1974 by Ernő Rubik, who also discovered an algorithm for finding good, but not optimal solutions.", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015F53", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Korf (1997)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "found optimal solutions", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000016433", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "for some random problem instances using pattern databases and IDA* search. Rokicki et al., (2014) proved that any instance can be solved in 26 moves (if you consider a twist to be", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "180∘ "}, {"type": "text", "content": "two moves; 20 if it counts as one). The proof consumed 35 CPU years of computation; it does not lead immediately to an efficient algorithm.", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015540", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Agostinelli et al. (2019)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "used reinforcement learning, deep learning networks, and Monte Carlo tree search to learn a much more efficient solver for Rubik’s cube. It is not guaranteed to find a cost-optimal solution, but does so about 60% of the time, and typical solutions times are less than a second.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}]}, {"page_idx": 227, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.139, 0.079, 0.844, 0.383], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Each of the real-world search problems listed in the chapter has been the subject of a good deal of research effort. Methods for selecting optimal airline flights remain proprietary for the most part, but Carl de Marcken has shown by a reduction to Diophantine decision problems that airline ticket pricing and restrictions have become so convoluted that the problem of selecting an optimal flight is formally undecidable (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001642D", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Robinson, 2002", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "). The traveling salesperson problem (TSP) is a standard combinatorial problem in theoretical computer science (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015FE8", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Lawler et al., 1992", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ").", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015E86", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Karp (1972)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "proved the TSP decision problem to be", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001604F", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "NP-hard, but effective heuristic approximation methods were developed (Lin and Kernighan, 1973).", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Arora (1998)", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001604F", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "devised a fully polynomial approximation scheme", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "for Euclidean TSPs. VLSI layout methods are surveyed by", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015FD0", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "LaPaugh (2010)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ", and many layout", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000014C8B.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000014C8B", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "optimization papers appear in VLSI journals. Robotic navigation is discussed in Chapter 26 . Automatic assembly sequencing was first demonstrated by FREDDY (", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "Michie, 1972", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000014C8B.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000014C8B", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ");", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "a", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "comprehensive review is given by (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000155F3", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Bahubalendruni and Biswal, 2016", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ").", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.139, 0.411, 0.857, 0.525], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Uninformed search algorithms are a central topic of computer science (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000158DC", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Cormen et al., 2009", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ") and operations research (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015A40", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Dreyfus, 1969", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "). Breadth-first search was formulated for solving", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015668", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "mazes by", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Moore (1959)", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015668", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ". The method of dynamic programming (", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "Bellman, 1957", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015668", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "; Bellman and Dreyfus, 1962), which systematically records solutions for all subproblems of increasing", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "lengths, can be seen as a form of breadth-first search.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.139, 0.554, 0.854, 0.739], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Dijkstra’s algorithm in the form it is usually presented in (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000159FA", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Dijkstra, 1959", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ") is applicable to explicit finite graphs.", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000016277", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Nilsson (1971)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "introduced a version of Dijkstra’s algorithm that he called uniform-cost search (because the algorithm “spreads out along contours of equal path cost”) that allows for implicitly defined, infinite graphs. Nilsson’s work also introduced the idea of closed and open lists, and the term “graph search.” The name BEST-FIRST-SEARCH was", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015B00", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "introduced in the Handbook of AI (", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "Barr and Feigenbaum, 1981", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015B00", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "). The Floyd–Warshall (Floyd, 1962) and Bellman-Ford (", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "Bellman, 1958", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015B00", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ";", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Ford, 1956", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015B00", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ") algorithms allow negative step costs", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "(as long as there are no negative cycles).", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.139, 0.767, 0.852, 0.857], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "A version of iterative deepening designed to make efficient use of the chess clock was first used by", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000165A2", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Slate and Atkin (1977)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "in the CHESS 4.5 game-playing program. Martelli’s algorithm B (1977) also includes an iterative deepening aspect. The iterative deepening technique was introduced by", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000163C9", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Bertram Raphael (1976)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "and came to the fore in work by", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015F43", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Korf (1985a)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ".", "styles": ["bold"]}]}]}, {"page_idx": 228, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.139, 0.08, 0.858, 0.311], "content": [{"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001658A", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The use of heuristic information in problem solving appears in an early paper by Simon and Newell (1958), but the phrase “heuristic search” and the use of heuristic functions that", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "estimate the distance to the goal came somewhat later (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001624C", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Newell and Ernst, 1965", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ";", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001604D", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Lin, 1965", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ").", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015A1A", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Doran and Michie (1966)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "conducted extensive experimental studies of heuristic search. Although they analyzed path length and “penetrance” (the ratio of path length to the total number of nodes examined so far), they appear to have ignored the information provided by the path cost", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " Theg(n). "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "A ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "algorithm, incorporating the current path cost into heuristic search, was developed by", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015CC9", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Hart, Nilsson, and Raphael (1968)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ".", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000159AB", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Dechter and Pearl (1985)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "studied the conditions under which", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\boldsymbol { A } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "is optimally efficient (in number of nodes expanded).", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.139, 0.34, 0.838, 0.405], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The original A* paper (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015CC9", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Hart et al., 1968", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ") introduced the consistency condition on heuristic functions. The monotone condition was introduced by", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000016364", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Pohl (1977)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "as a simpler replacement, but", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000162FB", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Pearl (1984)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "showed that the two were equivalent.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.139, 0.435, 0.856, 0.644], "content": [{"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000016364", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Pohl (1977)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "pioneered the study of the relationship between the error in heuristic functions and the time complexity of", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "A ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": ". Basic results were obtained for tree-like search with unit", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000162FB", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "action costs and a single goal state (", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "Pohl, 1977", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000162FB", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ";", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Gaschnig, 1979", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000162FB", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ";", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Huyn et al., 1980", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000162FB", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "; Pearl, 1984) and with multiple goal states (", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "Dinh et al., 2007", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000162FB", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ").", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Korf and Reid (1998)", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000162FB", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "showed ho", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "w to predict the exact number of nodes expanded (not just an asymptotic approximation) on a", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000016277", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "variety of actual problem domains. The “effective branching factor” was proposed by Nilsson (1971) as an empirical measure of efficiency. For graph search,", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Helmert and Röger (2008) noted that several well-known problems contained exponentially many nodes on optimal", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " cost solution paths, implying exponential time complexity for "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "A ^ { * }"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.139, 0.672, 0.853, 0.881], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "There are many variations on the A* algorithm. Pohl (1970b) introduced weighted A* search, and later a dynamic version (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000016362", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "1973", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "), where the weight changes over the depth of the tree.", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015A65", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Ebendt and Drechsler (2009)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "synthesize the results and examine some applications.", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015CDF", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Hatem and Ruml (2014)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "show a simplified and improved version of weighted", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "A ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "that is easier to implement.", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000167CE", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Wilt and Ruml (2014)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "introduce speedy search as an alternative to greedy search that focuses on minimizing search time, and", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000167D0", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Wilt and Ruml (2016)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "show that", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000157D0", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "the best heuristics for satisficing search are different from the ones for optimal search. Burns et al. (2012) give some implementation tricks for writing fast search code, and", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Felner (2018) considers how the implementation changes when using an early goal test.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}]}, {"page_idx": 229, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.139, 0.079, 0.854, 0.193], "content": [{"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000016360", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Pohl (1971)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "introduced bidirectional search.", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015D68", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Holte et al. (2016)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "describe the version of bidirectional search that is guaranteed to meet in the middle, making it more widely applicable. (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015A67", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "EckerLe et al. 2017", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ") describe the set of surely expanded pairs of nodes, and", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015853", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "show that no bidirectional search can be optimally efficient. The NBS algorithm (Chen et al., 2017) makes explicit use of a queue of pairs of nodes.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.139, 0.221, 0.856, 0.501], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "A combination of bidirectional A* and known landmarks was used to efficiently find driving routes for Microsoft’s online map service (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015C0B", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Goldberg et al., 2006", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "). After caching a set of paths between landmarks, the algorithm can find an optimal-cost path between any pair of points", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015F47", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "in a 24-million-point graph of the United States, searching less than 0.1% of the graph. Korf (1987) shows how to use subgoals, macro-operators, and abstraction to achieve remarkable", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "speedups over previous techniques.", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000159CE", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Delling et al. (2009)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "describe how to use bidirectional", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015574", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "search, landmarks, hierarchical structure, and other tricks to find driving routes. Anderson et al. (2008) describe a related technique, called coarse-to-fine search, which can be thought", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "of as defining landmarks at various hierarchical levels of abstraction.", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015F47", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Korf (1987)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "describes", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015EF2", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "conditions under which coarse-to-fine search provides an exponential speedup. Knoblock (1991) provides experimental results and analysis to quantify the advantages of hierarchic", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "al search.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.218, 0.567, 0.376, 0.584], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Coarse-to-fine search", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.139, 0.65, 0.855, 0.764], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "A* and other state-space search algorithms are closely related to the branch-and-bound", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000163E1", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "techniques that are widely used in operations research (", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "Lawler and Wood, 1966", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000163E1", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "; Rayward-Smith et al., 1996).", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Kumar and Kanal (1988)", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000163E1", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "attempt a “grand unification” of heuristic searc", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "h, dynamic programming, and branch-and-bound techniques under the name of CDP—the “composite decision process.”", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "footer", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.218, 0.83, 0.364, 0.846], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Branch-and-bound"}]}]}], "is_full_document": false, "schema": "docvortex.middle", "schema_version": "2.0"}